# Each phrase is a chat reply's framing, which says nothing about the
# subject. The Recommended rule of the same name holds the phrases that
# human-written READMEs also use. `of course` sits in the filler rule,
# so it draws one finding.
#
# Weir reads `!` as negation, so a pattern cannot name a literal
# exclamation mark. `certainly` and `absolutely` count instead when no
# space or other punctuation follows them, which leaves the `!` of a
# chat opener (`Certainly! Here is the fix`). The guard sits glued to
# the word so it reads the token right after it. A closing paren cannot
# sit in the list, because it ends the list. `(absolutely)` therefore
# still fires, and so does the word at the end of a line.
#
# `you're right` counts only with punctuation glued after it (`You're
# right, the cache was stale`), which leaves `you're right-clicking`
# and `you're right next to the limit`. `good catch` and `great catch`
# skip a following code word (`a good catch block`). The phrase
# `here's the updated` and its kin return a reply to the asker.
#
# `apologies for`, `I apologize`, and `sorry for the confusion` answer
# a complaint, and `excellent question` and `glad you asked` praise the
# asker. `that's a good point` and `you're welcome` count only with
# punctuation glued after them, the way `you're right` does. That
# guard passes `a good point to set a breakpoint` and `you are welcome
# to open an issue`.
#
# It hit 1 line in the measure.py corpus, and 0 of 1 sampled hits was a
# chat reply. The miss asked the reader to test a guess (`check to see
# if you're right!`). The phrases for apologies and praise hit none.
expr code [block, blocks, clause, clauses, statement, statements, handler, handlers, arm, arms]
expr main [(hope this helps), (hope that helps), (hope [this, that] [clarifies, clears]), (great question), (good question), ([great, good] catch!(( )@code)), ([you're, (you are)] right!([( ), -, /])), ([here's, (here is), (here are)] the [updated, revised, corrected, fixed]), (you're absolutely right), (you are absolutely right), (I'd be happy to), (I would be happy to), ([certainly, absolutely]![( ), ,, ., ?, ;, :, -, /]), (apologies for), (I apologize), (sorry for the confusion), ([(that's), (that is)] a [great, good, fair, valid, excellent] point![( ), -, /]), ([excellent, fantastic] question), ([(you're), (you are)] welcome![( ), -, /]), (glad you asked)]
let message "Chatbot phrase. Delete it and respond directly."
let description "Flags phrases that frame a reply as a chat instead of stating the point."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The absolutely-positioned element overlaps the header."
allows "The fix is certainly correct."
allows "Is it safe? Certainly."
allows "A good catch block logs the error before it rethrows."
allows "Make sure you're right-clicking the tray icon."
allows "That is a good point to set a breakpoint."
allows "You are welcome to open an issue."
